From 52fc366431432677d6839643fcad54515a0d6fee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arya Alikhani <alexanderaryaalikhani@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:12:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/17] boringssl: negotiate record_size_limit over QUIC

Firefox sends record_size_limit 16385 in every QUIC ClientHello, and
neqo echoes the extension in EncryptedExtensions. Patch 0005 refused a
non-zero limit on any QUIC connection: in both setters, in both
SSL_set_quic_method variants, at the start of each handshake, and in
the ClientHello writer.

None of those refusals protects anything. QUIC carries handshake
messages in CRYPTO frames through add_handshake_data and installs no
TLS record state for 0-RTT, and only the TLS record layer reads the
negotiated limits. RFC 9001 section 3 says QUIC takes over the
responsibilities of the record layer, so the extension has no effect,
but nothing in RFC 9001 forbids sending or negotiating it.

Let QUIC clients and servers configure a limit and negotiate the
extension; DTLS and handoff stay refused. A QUIC server echoes its
limit like a TLS server, so ssl_test and loopback servers can exercise
the client's echo path. Because QUIC 0-RTT does not travel in TLS
records, a QUIC server's limit no longer keeps it from issuing
early-data tickets or accepting early data.
